.TH COFFER 1 "2026-11-01" "coffer" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
coffer \- headless PTY inspector for the coffer VT engine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B coffer
[\fI\,options\/\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fI\,command args...\/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B coffer
spawns a child process on a pseudo-terminal, pipes its output through
the coffer VT engine, and renders the resulting grid as plain text.
It needs no display server, so it is suited to debugging VT rendering,
capturing raw PTY output, inspecting individual cells and styles, and
asserting on what a TUI drew in CI.
.PP
The engine's output callback is wired back to the PTY, so terminal
queries (DA, DSR, kitty keyboard protocol) receive proper replies and
interactive programs render as they would in a real terminal.
.PP
Everything after
.B \-\-
is the command to run.
Without one,
.B coffer
starts
.B $SHELL
(falling back to
.BR /bin/sh ;
.B %COMSPEC%
or
.B cmd.exe
on Windows).
.PP
A run goes through these steps:
.IP 1. 3
Spawn the child on a PTY of the requested size.
.IP 2. 3
Drain its output for the initial wait
.RB ( \-\-wait ).
.IP 3. 3
Execute the script, if
.B \-\-script
was given.
.IP 4. 3
Dump the final screen (see
.BR "FINAL DUMP" ),
unless the script failed.
.IP 5. 3
Kill the child if it is still running, and exit.
.PP
Script assertions and
.B wait\-for
search the
.B unified buffer
\(em the scrollback (oldest first) followed by the visible rows \(em so
a TUI whose transcript scrolls off the screen is still assertable.
.PP
Progress and diagnostics (the startup notice, per-command
.B OK
/
.B FAIL
lines, script errors) go to standard error.
Grid dumps go to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
Options must precede
.BR \-\- ;
short options cannot be combined.
.TP
\fB\-\-rows\fR, \fB\-r\fR \fIROWS\fR
Terminal rows (default: 24).
.TP
\fB\-\-cols\fR, \fB\-c\fR \fICOLS\fR
Terminal columns (default: 80).
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\fR, \fB\-w\fR \fISEC\fR
Initial wait in seconds before the script runs or the screen is
rendered (default: 3; accepts decimals).
The wait always happens, with or without a script; a script that
synchronizes with
.B wait\-for
can pass a small value.
.TP
\fB\-\-script\fR, \fB\-f\fR \fIFILE\fR
Execute commands from a script file. See
.B SCRIPT FILE
below.
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR
Save raw PTY output to
.IR FILE .
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-row\fR, \fB\-s\fR \fIROW\fR
Select a row for the final dump (repeatable, up to 64).
Without
.BR \-\-quiet ,
the full screen is still printed and
.B \-\-show\-row
only restricts the
.B \-\-cell\-data
and
.B \-\-style\-data
detail to the selected rows.
.TP
\fB\-\-cell\-data\fR, \fB\-d\fR
After the final dump, print per-cell detail (codepoint, width, style,
hyperlink id), as
.B render cells
does, for every row or for the rows selected with
.BR \-\-show\-row .
.TP
\fB\-\-style\-data\fR, \fB\-S\fR
After the final dump, print style detail (underline, colors,
attributes), as
.B render styles
does, for every row or the selected rows.
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR, \fB\-q\fR
With
.BR \-\-show\-row ,
print only the selected rows rather than the whole screen.
Has no effect without
.BR \-\-show\-row .
.TP
\fB\-\-env\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR
Set an environment variable for the child (repeatable, up to 64).
Applied after the built-in
.B TERM
/
.B COLORTERM
/
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ;
an empty
.I VALUE
unsets the variable, including a built-in.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-h\fR
Show help (options and script commands) and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\-spec\fR
Emit a machine-readable, tab-separated listing for downstream tooling
and exit.
See
.BR "FEATURE DETECTION" .
.SH FINAL DUMP
When no script is given, or the script finishes without failing
(including via
.BR quit ),
.B coffer
renders the visible grid to standard output after the script, one row
per line as
.RI \(dq "ROW" | "TEXT" \(dq
(the same output as a bare
.BR render ).
.B \-\-show\-row ,
.BR \-\-cell\-data ,
.B \-\-style\-data
and
.B \-\-quiet
shape this dump only; they do not affect
.B render
commands inside a script.
When the script fails, the dump is skipped and the exit status is 1.
.SH SCRIPT FILE
When run with
.BR \-\-script ,
each line is one command; blank lines and lines whose first
non-blank character is
.B #
are ignored.
A line may be at most 4095 bytes long.
A parse error (unknown command, bad arguments) is reported with its line
number and fails the run before the script starts.
The script engine is part of the coffer library
.RI ( <coffer/script.h> )
and is shared with other hosts, which may register extra commands.
.PP
Commands that take a
.I TEXT
argument accept it with or without one layer of surrounding single or
double quotes.
.TP
.BI "wait " SECONDS
Drain PTY output for
.I SECONDS
seconds (accepts decimals; a missing or non-positive value means 1).
.TP
.BI "wait\-for " "\(dqTEXT\(dq [TIMEOUT] [line-end]"
Drain until
.I TEXT
appears in the unified buffer, polling as output arrives.
.I TIMEOUT
is in seconds (default: 600); reaching it is a failure, so the script
fails rather than hangs.
The needle is matched as UTF-8 and has backslash escapes expanded (see
.BR send ).
The optional trailing
.B line-end
keyword anchors the match to a row that ends with
.IR TEXT .
.IP
.I TIMEOUT
and
.B line-end
are trailing tokens, peeled off the end of the line right to left (at
most one number); anything else is part of the needle.
Because a match is made against a row's text, a needle that spans a
soft wrap will not be found \(em use a shorter one.
.TP
.BI "send " TEXT
Write
.I TEXT
to the PTY input, that is, the child's standard input.
The bytes are
.I not
fed to coffer's parser: to make the terminal process an escape
sequence, have the child print it.
Backslash escapes are expanded:
.B \en
= LF,
.B \er
= CR,
.B \ee
= ESC,
.B \et
= TAB,
.B \exNN
= byte NN (hex),
.B \e\e
= backslash.
Octal escapes such as
.B \e033
are deliberately
.I not
expanded; write
.BR \ee .
A malformed
.B \ex
keeps its backslash.
A shell only runs what you type once it sees a newline, so end shell
commands with
.BR \en .
.TP
.BI "sendln " TEXT
Like
.BR send ,
with a trailing CR LF.
.TP
.BI "raw " "HEX [HEX ...]"
Write literal bytes, given as space-separated hex pairs, to the PTY
input (e.g.\&
.B raw 03
for Ctrl-C,
.B raw ff fc 01
for a telnet IAC WONT ECHO).
.TP
.BI "emit " TEXT
Feed
.I TEXT
straight into coffer's terminal parser, bypassing the child (same
escape expansion as
.BR send ).
.TP
.BI "emit\-raw " "HEX [HEX ...]"
Feed literal bytes straight into coffer's parser.
.TP
.BI "assert\-contains " TEXT
Fail the run if the unified buffer does not contain
.IR TEXT .
The text is matched as UTF-8 and, unlike
.BR wait\-for ,
is taken literally: no escape expansion.
.TP
.BI "assert\-not\-contains " TEXT
Fail the run if the unified buffer contains
.IR TEXT .
.TP
.BI "await\-exit " "[TIMEOUT]"
Drain until the child exits (default timeout: 10 seconds; a timeout is
a failure) and record its exit status.
.TP
.BI "assert\-exit " STATUS
Fail the run unless
.B await\-exit
recorded
.IR STATUS .
It is also a failure if no
.B await\-exit
ran first.
.TP
.BI "resize " "COLS ROWS"
Resize the PTY and the grid; both must be positive.
On POSIX the size is set on the child's terminal with
.BR TIOCSWINSZ ,
so it receives
.BR SIGWINCH ;
on Windows
.B ResizePseudoConsole
is used (Windows 10 1809 or later; under wine it is unimplemented and
the command fails).
.TP
.BI "render " "[visible|scrollback|all]"
Dump the grid (default:
.BR visible ).
Each row is printed as
.IB ROW | TEXT\fR.
.B scrollback
prints the scrollback, oldest first, with unified row numbers (negative
= scrollback depth), or
.B (scrollback empty)\fR;
.B all
prints the scrollback followed by the visible rows.
.TP
.BI "render cells " "ROW [C0 C1]"
Dump per-cell detail (codepoint, width, attributes, colors, underline,
hyperlink id) for
.IR ROW ,
columns
.I C0
through
.I C1
(default: the whole row).
Rows are unified coordinates: negative values address the scrollback
.RB ( \-1
= most recently scrolled off).
.TP
.BI "render styles " ROW
Dump style detail (underline, colors, attributes) for the cells of
.I ROW
that carry any.
.TP
.B cursor
Dump the cursor state (row, col, visible, blink).
.TP
.B dump\-sixel
Dump live images (id, anchor row/col, pixel size) and their placements.
Coffer assumes a 10x6 pixel cell, which determines the cell footprint
of a sixel.
.TP
.B quit
End the script run early, successfully.
The final dump still happens.
.TP
.BI "# " comment
Ignored.
.SH FEATURE DETECTION
.B \-\-help\-spec
prints one line per option and one per script command, with tab-separated
fields and
.B \-
standing for an absent value:
.PP
.EX
FLAG	NAME	ALIAS	TAKES_VALUE	PARAM	DESCRIPTION
CMD	NAME	ARGS	DESCRIPTION
.EE
.PP
.B render cells
and
.B render styles
get their own
.B CMD
lines.
A driver that needs a command greps for
.B CMD<TAB>NAME
and skips when the installed binary predates it, instead of guessing
from a version number.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Capture the initial screen of crush:
.EX
coffer \-c 68 \-r 20 \-w 5 \-\- crush
.EE
.TP
Inspect one row's cells, hiding the rest of the screen:
.EX
coffer \-c 68 \-r 20 \-s 18 \-d \-q \-\- crush
.EE
.TP
Capture raw PTY output for offline replay:
.EX
coffer \-o /tmp/crush.raw \-\- crush
.EE
.TP
Scripted CI test with assertions:
.EX
coffer \-r 24 \-c 80 \-w 0.5 \-f test.script \-\- sh \-c "echo hello world"
.EE
where
.I test.script
might be:
.EX
wait\-for "hello world" 5
assert\-contains "hello world"
await\-exit 5
assert\-exit 0
.EE
.TP
Drive an interactive shell and resize mid-run:
.EX
wait\-for "$ " 10
send stty size\en
wait\-for "24 80" 5
resize 120 40
send stty size\en
wait\-for "40 120" 5
.EE
.TP
Make the terminal handle an escape sequence by having the child print
it (\fBsend\fR itself goes to the child's stdin, and the shell's
readline would swallow a literal ESC):
.EX
send printf '\e033[?25l'\en
wait 1
cursor
.EE
.TP
Pin a color tier by unsetting the built-in
.BR COLORTERM :
.EX
coffer \-\-env COLORTERM= \-\- my\-tui
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; all assertions passed (if any).
.TP
.B 1
An assertion failed, a
.B wait\-for
or
.B await\-exit
timed out, the script could not be read or parsed, an option was
invalid, or another runtime error occurred.
.PP
The exit status of the child is not propagated; use
.B await\-exit
with
.B assert\-exit
to check it.
A child that cannot be executed exits with status 127 inside the PTY.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
The child process sees
.BR xterm\-256color .
.TP
.B COLORTERM
The child process sees
.BR truecolor .
.TP
.B TERM_PROGRAM
The child process sees
.BR ghostty .
.TP
.B SHELL
The program started when no command is given (POSIX).
.PP
The first three can be overridden with
.BR \-\-env .
.SH PLATFORMS
POSIX systems use
.BR forkpty (3).
Windows (MSYS2 UCRT64) uses ConPTY, with a background thread reading
the pseudo-console output pipe.
.SH BUGS
On Windows, a
.B \-\-env
override is silently dropped if it has no
.BR = ,
its key is 128 characters or longer, or its value is 512 or longer.
.PP
No interactive input \(em all input is scripted via
.BR \-\-script .
No mouse support.
.PP
.B assert\-contains
and
.B assert\-not\-contains
search the whole unified buffer (scrollback included), not just the
visible screen; that is a change from the visible-only search of
earlier releases.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty (7),
.BR forkpty (3)
.PP
.I docs/coffer-cli.md
and
.I include/coffer/script.h
in the coffer source tree.
